#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087425 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd63cb30-a236-546e-82ac-08bce09c88f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "8ab1df94-0c4f-5720-a9b6-939dba0ab9c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-1219" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=4^{x}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y=4^{x}{:}$\n\n![](images/df/dfac014ecedaa03a46048b4729ddfa60e7a3776322d0b9ea19035fbcd24bf85b.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y=4^x-1,$ we take the graph of $y=4^x$ and shift it $1$ unit down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-1.$\n\n![](images/ee/eef6c5dd67c683ccd0826caa9760fa240be973f8debd29b41d463f6184a00620.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087426 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79daf9dd-814d-562b-b3bc-b066bd2dfb90" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "65c814f8-df2f-549d-9467-c6dda6890bb3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1220" :question/problem "The graph below shows the curve $y=3^{x}+c$ . What is the value of $c?$\n\n![](images/00/00264eb90dbaa2c50fdd626d61292c0428fd0bbbd03a6d051353598450b4ad89.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y=3^x$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y=3^x+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$ Therefore, $c=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087427 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f73207f4-4661-55f1-acfb-cc5fe6ba9316" :question/math-academy-id "e-1221" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y=3^{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/1c/1cd695e6f1fab35a692fde9003d452862db33a894c23657eb87f8d773de8cbb9.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $5.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c92ea5f3cbfd40bd0dc384b152f6166c27400dbedd93a9c8333195950e7f9c3.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,5)$ to $(0,2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8e8cdccd58a9f1f21c8c2bf0a4a8c08e9c263c4fe91cc92e2bee334de057116.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087428 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "62f7d6e1-61ec-5f3d-8cdd-4da0c90c7ccf" :question/math-academy-id "e-1222" :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=k(6^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](images/de/deb7e20658882229a3653f4058c787bf2a5c4a26c5f2982406a2dba34c2d5846.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k(6^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4$, so $c=4.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k(6^{x})+4.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({6}^{x})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k({6}^{0})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k(1)+4 \\\\ 6 & =k+4 \\\\ 2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=2(6^{x})+4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40cd7eb5-26eb-5f47-9864-d179eee20251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2.5({2}^{x})-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531001} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2740d1-15f5-5c10-9d5d-f42781190300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531023} {:answer/id #uuid "71407824-a8ad-5e1e-b6fe-b9ba8075671b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2.5({2}^{x})+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531117} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e9d21b-a213-54e5-93ab-0172b47154ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531247} {:answer/id #uuid "e50c8d48-2425-5e73-bd1c-d2a842248e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0.5({2}^{x})+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71407824-a8ad-5e1e-b6fe-b9ba8075671b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2.5({2}^{x})+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531117} :answer-field/id #uuid "d29ba8d5-82d0-52b6-939b-119fe78d8db4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8412e9ee-2ea9-5393-944f-a1233b5c49f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-18519" :question/problem "![](images/95/9507e060ddd7de93489b236b48856870e2c884eba69f63ac67662dfd1c061c17.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1,$ so $c=1.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})+1.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,3.5),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=3.5$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})+1 \\\\ 3.5 & =k({2}^{0})+1 \\\\ 3.5 & =k(1)+1 \\\\ 3.5 & =k+1 \\\\ 2.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=2.5({2}^{x})+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd63cb30-a236-546e-82ac-08bce09c88f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80805f9a-da2c-5f09-95bf-39e85749a157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b8/b87a0e69a4778398ee8d777997b6c17a29107db011e61f60cc7e96a7564b4e08.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085291} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2eb4dc-374c-563e-84cf-cf2c6be18a16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b582a60c7be67ad776df098cb2e5cd54083aec78a400d3cb5f06f51fbb949988.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085292} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b8e4b9-7a4c-571e-9c28-d33267dcdb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6f2ebca29087546872303988d034183b634286da680fcd2245f3fe063a300efe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085293} {:answer/id #uuid "2519e987-ba87-5028-bb61-2b4887e0e5b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4f/4fe9f375fc6adb646e282f7b62171b9df5d564194dfcde3d07210a2a74a11ca1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085294} {:ans