,$ we take the graph of $y={7}^{x}$ and shift it $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/85/85cc1403ab74e2df97669a7c3363d1abddf6d093c358e888a69b655f1ca7450a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0586aaf8-5b17-5e90-a5da-6384fff65d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/05/052daab57bdb99d53853e8d7d2cc7a059f9f24f715b1340857407d5a44c68acd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530871} {:answer/id #uuid "200914d3-6a9d-52ed-b42e-4c7f306d6701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/32/32a5e6e0c5c6efc2eb44f67d208c1abbe43dfeb86a30b1fbcd77388268766351.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530930} {:answer/id #uuid "62bc53be-cfce-539e-8c16-62df3a417995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ea/ea936fd09ea4fca3ccd90d07bc3d1b5710629f3907fdc3d3185e708ff0919a6d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531079} {:answer/id #uuid "793b9e2c-b5e0-5f42-a1eb-e1c2d16e9f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f1/f1b7d59439cf1a272795815cb8f8e0b6dd0f5ea45038d4cb34bbdf368db9539c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531132} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdb1675-ded2-5d85-bf36-c30f6bad3b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/07/07d639972162c1601c0fa094f874a82b85fdeb22f58cdb15d92c9268ac0d2aca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0586aaf8-5b17-5e90-a5da-6384fff65d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/05/052daab57bdb99d53853e8d7d2cc7a059f9f24f715b1340857407d5a44c68acd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530871} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaab585b-caae-5298-a3ee-f10042b952cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bfc4a3c-5e7b-532b-95bd-4e49a1efa533" :question/math-academy-id "q-26914" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4({2}^{x})+4?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={2}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/6d/6d5b359544227b89d1dc5ca9967c9dfcda84bf5345184063a1d0deef994c3692.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=4({2}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,4),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/86/86b10c71101ae46d4c99335874eb3cd75abc416c0d9a3b4552f6f6a52644aa2f.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=4({2}^{x})+4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,4)$ to $(0,8),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/05/052daab57bdb99d53853e8d7d2cc7a059f9f24f715b1340857407d5a44c68acd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165e4770-3b92-5d99-9106-610397e6128e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a3daf867442355a2c0d77796f4c0c663c885a63012a758b9f99cfa2ef1ed2aca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530906} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd4c895-f750-5a06-9cea-e6f503cc0670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/7763b1d82df8d07d26911327ea4f900764c83ea632a654d84a0adab7f47f3ad1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530994} {:answer/id #uuid "48db64b8-d987-5b2a-8b10-92b11a967cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dd/dd405f0b3fde6b4fa4128afec90dba1508935e1544a57f642c70809843d9b220.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531019} {:answer/id #uuid "70ff0af0-9e57-50f8-8391-0720265b0dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/57ac1f2daa7ee44b5cf9fbd2d4c9ce8a25edc3a3053af4d9164b7f8e83b9e00f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531116} {:answer/id #uuid "93c0a0ea-146f-5cdb-a4df-6753aab6927f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/08/08572884c3dc95c13b37b2044fac25d9da663e79f9b71fc92fbc7e227e1a3360.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70ff0af0-9e57-50f8-8391-0720265b0dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/57ac1f2daa7ee44b5cf9fbd2d4c9ce8a25edc3a3053af4d9164b7f8e83b9e00f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531116} :answer-field/id #uuid "a15d6ff2-97c9-5cc7-84c4-220104b8fcdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be426e06-620a-5f1c-9841-393e8911826d" :question/math-academy-id "q-26915" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=6({5}^{x})+4?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={5}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/84/8490920052778795ca03072fd0a6933b4abc5756f2e75ae9f61863d3726dd5c8.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=6({5}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $6.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,6),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/b5/b5deca2c07bc8c8a5e58c28a6ca2449268999a54b21407380ae168aafabd7a5d.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=6({5}^{x})+4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,6)$ to $(0,10),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/57/57ac1f2daa7ee44b5cf9fbd2d4c9ce8a25edc3a3053af4d9164b7f8e83b9e00f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8870de-643a-5fb2-8463-3f97e48224cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}({10}^{x})-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530884} {:answer/id #uuid "38db0a76-5e16-5aba-bb93-35df5dab3d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4({10}^{x})-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530987} {:answer/id #uuid "620577fa-0f5e-5e33-9265-a1ff721c4a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={10}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531077} {:answer/id #uuid "807ef638-7e9d-572d-846b-e9a5c5315681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={10}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531142} {:answer/id #uuid "b47e9017-f47b-5637-b27e-af639f76e073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4({10}^{x})+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38db0a76-5e16-5aba-bb93-35df5dab3d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4({10}^{x})-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530987} :answer-field/id #uuid "c65486dd-75a5-525a-b8ec-541d0f93ec2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33e5e3ff-cf8e-5556-9914-9fd8f6506ea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-30282" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9a448574651a74ef7180549827e05ee2d790cd9fa89c6c85adf08d8444ceaadf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({10}^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({10}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3,$ so $c=-3.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({10}^{x})-3.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({10}^{x})-3 \\\\ 1 & =k({10}^{0})-3 \\\\ 1 & =k(1)-3 \\\\ 1 & =k-3 \\\\ 4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=4({10}^{x})-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79daf9dd-814d-562b-b3bc-b066bd2dfb90" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41a10cf8-fa76-5d2c-bbfa-53fc9fd443d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={7}^{x}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361128} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e2e43d-b922-5fc1-8f77-36bc7f400904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5({7}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361565} {:answer/id #uuid "af0858d3-eaab-52b2-97be-a12e1d91a0e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={7}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361611} {:answer/id #uuid "d69de0a5-dc56-545f-b406-c4a613d3a95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5({7}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361793} {:answer/id #uuid "e22e67b0-ee2f-534d-84c4-3a12b2bccb58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={7}^{x}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e22e67b0-ee2f-534d-84c4-3a12b2bccb58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={7}^{x}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361848} :answer-field/id #uuid "45e82ee6-d033-5a82-bd35-1a20f7830c09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b018fdf1-c3ab-56d5-97f8-9a97dabdfd94" :question/math-academy-id "q-99774" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d80c45f952e4c8a65349be524849104b1a76e6ee276186646c6c3facd83c8181.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={7}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={7}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={7}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5.$ Therefore, $c=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79daf9dd-814d-562b-b3bc-b066bd2dfb90" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15df6806-2868-52a1-bb87-ccc3b49c71eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3({12}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360925} {:answer/id #uuid "25ac3197-829d-5e42-82e7-ccbe433ae931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={12}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360999} {:answer/id #uuid "320d2665-a93e-59a0-94f1-05e1cac58508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={12}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361058} {:answer/id #uuid "ca540824-50d3-5d23-8a1f-a357a7ac4306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({12}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361750} {:answer/id #uuid "dfca9065-c86b-5bbf-8b4b-f41f1e5b6271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={12}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "320d2665-a93e-59a0-94f1-05e1cac58508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={12}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361058} :answer-field/id #uuid "b04a0e13-1fa0-5663-98fb-aa7fb24bee49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8e58eb8-2e35-57c5-8905-f16a49b3cc0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99775" :question/problem "![](images/92/929706c015fd38e83c9529a28203584a4e3e53a204ac3b4d20539d090a3fd25a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={12}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={2}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={12}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$ Therefore, $c=-3.$"}]}
